SoundGuide: {
	Title: Ariadne Sound Guide
	Summary: Hear every sound Ariadne makes and what each one tells you.
	Label: "{0}: {1}"
	VariantPosition: Variant {0} of {1}.
	ControlHint: Enter: play
	Instructions: Press Enter to play the sound the focused option names. Each press then moves that option on to its next variant and says which one, so pressing Enter repeatedly hears all of them. Left and Right Arrow keys choose a variant without playing it. Everything plays at the volumes you have set, so this is what the world will sound like.
	Unavailable: Nothing can be played because Ariadne's audio bus is unavailable. See the tModLoader client log.
	GameVolumeOff: Nothing will be heard until Terraria's Sound volume is raised above zero.
	CueVolumeOff: Nothing will be heard until this cue's volume is raised above zero in Ariadne's settings.

	Help: {
		Enter: Play the sound the focused option names, then move that option on to its next variant and speak it. Pressing Enter repeatedly walks every variant of a cue and returns to the first. What is spoken after each press is what the next press will play.
		VariantsControl: Left and Right Arrow keys
		Variants: Choose which variant of the focused cue Enter will play. The variant is spoken but nothing is played, so you can pick one deliberately instead of stepping through them.
		VolumesControl: Volumes
		Volumes: Every cue plays at the loudness you have configured for it, underneath Terraria's own Sound volume. A cue turned down to zero stays silent here too, and the focused option says so rather than leaving you with silence to interpret.
	}

	Cues: {
		Footsteps: {
			Name: Footsteps
			Description: A short thud each time you walk onto a new tile, so movement has a rhythm and standing still is silent. Four timbres are used in rotation, which keeps a long walk from becoming one repeating click, and the pitch names what you are standing on. Mounts, teleports and grapples are deliberately silent.

			Variants: {
				Ground: {
					Name: Solid ground
					Detail: The neutral step. Four of them play here at a walking pace, which is what shows the rotation between timbres.
				}

				Platform: {
					Name: Wooden platform
					Detail: A perfect fourth above solid ground: far enough to name without listening for the interval, and close enough to still be a footstep. Every tile holding you up has to be a platform, because one solid tile underfoot would stop the descent the raised pitch promises.
				}
			}
		}

		MovementBumps: {
			Name: Blocked movement
			Description: A low knock whenever a movement request stops making progress. It rings about three times as long as a footstep, so the two never blur together inside one stride, and it sounds from you rather than from the surface. Momentum on ice, knockback, mounts, ropes and grapples are all excluded, so it only answers when the controls are genuinely being refused.

			Variants: {
				Wall: {
					Name: Wall ahead
					Detail: The neutral knock: terrain in the direction you are walking, damped into a dull thud so it cannot be mistaken for a step.
				}

				Door: {
					Name: Closed door ahead
					Detail: A fifth higher, dying away faster, with the partial pushed up and the noise pulled back so it reads as a knock on wood. Sounding the same as stone made every closed door read as a dead end.
				}

				Landing: {
					Name: Landing
					Detail: Pitched down. It grows louder with the height of the drop up to about fifteen tiles; this is a full-weight landing.
				}

				Ceiling: {
					Name: Head against the ceiling
					Detail: Pitched up, the mirror of a landing.
				}
			}
		}

		Heartbeat: {
			Name: Low health heartbeat
			Description: A two-part lub-dub that begins once health falls to half and quickens at each lower threshold, so how hurt you are is legible without reading the life meter. Raising the pitch also tightens the gap between the two thumps, the way a quickening pulse does. Four beats play for each stage, because the rate is most of what a stage means.

			Variants: {
				Half: {
					Name: Half health
					Detail: About thirty-six beats a minute, the slowest stage and the one that starts the cue.
				}

				Forty: {
					Name: Forty percent health
					Detail: About forty-four beats a minute.
				}

				Thirty: {
					Name: Thirty percent health
					Detail: About fifty-five beats a minute.
				}

				Twenty: {
					Name: Twenty percent health
					Detail: About sixty-nine beats a minute.
				}

				Ten: {
					Name: Ten percent health
					Detail: About ninety beats a minute. This is where the spoken warning changes from low to critical.
				}

				Five: {
					Name: Five percent health
					Detail: About a hundred and twenty beats a minute, the fastest stage.
				}
			}
		}

		CursorEarcons: {
			Name: Cursor earcons
			Description: Every step of the unlocked cursor onto a nonempty tile plays that tile's own Terraria sound, from the tile's direction and distance rather than from you, so the shape of what you are moving across is audible. Each clip is levelled to one shared loudness first, because here level has to mean distance and nothing about the tile. Smart Cursor sounds one whenever its resolved target changes. They are auditioned from directly in front of you; in play the cue sits wherever the cursor is.

			Variants: {
				Stone: {
					Name: Stone and dirt
					Detail: Terraria's dig sound. Its three variants are used in rotation, so press Enter more than once to hear them.
				}

				Grass: {
					Name: Grass
					Detail: A single sound with no variants.
				}

				Metal: {
					Name: Metal ore
					Detail: The tink of a tile too hard for the pickaxe in hand, also in three variants.
				}

				Glass: {
					Name: Glass and shatter
					Detail: A single sound with no variants.
				}

				Water: {
					Name: Water and honey
					Detail: A liquid alternates a strong and a weak splash, so two consecutive steps through the same pool are never identical.
				}

				Shimmer: {
					Name: Shimmer
					Detail: Shimmer has four dedicated splashes of its own rather than sharing water's.
				}

				Lava: {
					Name: Lava
					Detail: The only dedicated lava sound Terraria exposes is its flow, so the cue is a faded first second of it.
				}
			}
		}

		CombatTarget: {
			Name: Combat target cues
			Description: Marks a combat target being taken and lost, and whether the target you already hold can be hurt at this moment. All four share one timbre and one envelope, so only the direction of the pitch sweep says which event it is, and a rise against a fall is about the most reliable distinction hearing offers. In play each sounds from the target itself, so a loss comes from where the target last was and still names the direction of the thing that vanished.

			Variants: {
				Acquired: {
					Name: Target taken
					Detail: A rise between two pitches.
				}

				Lost: {
					Name: Target lost
					Detail: The same two pitches falling, given slightly longer because a descent needs room to read as settling rather than as being cut off.
				}

				Opened: {
					Name: Target can be hit
					Detail: The rise an octave up and about half as long. A boss part whose window is a fraction of a second cannot be reported in words, so this is what carries it.
				}

				Shielded: {
					Name: Target cannot be hurt
					Detail: The fall an octave up. Moon Lord's hands answer this several times a second, which is why the pair is kept short and pitched well clear of the one it must not be mistaken for.
				}
			}
		}

		Radar: {
			Name: Radar
			Description: Sounds a struck bell for anything the scanner would list when it first comes into range, so you know when opening the scanner is worth it. It says nothing while nothing new arrives. What was found is carried by how many times the bell is struck, because pitch already means height here and every band is already spoken for by another cue. The timbre is inharmonic, which is what makes it read as struck rather than played and is the one thing in the mix built that way.

			Variants: {
				Ore: {
					Name: Ore or valuable
					Detail: One strike. The ground a Spelunker Potion would highlight.
				}

				Container: {
					Name: Container
					Detail: Two strikes. A chest, barrel or dresser.
				}

				Creature: {
					Name: Creature
					Detail: Three strikes. The town, rescuable and passive creatures a Hunter Potion would highlight.
				}

				Other: {
					Name: Anything else armed
					Detail: Four strikes. Whatever else you have switched on: enemies, dropped items, liquids, trees or placed objects.
				}

				Distant: {
					Name: Ore, far to your right
					Detail: The one-strike ping quiet and hard right. Nothing but loudness has changed, because nothing but loudness carries distance.
				}
			}
		}

		HostileMobTones: {
			Name: Hostile enemy tone
			Description: One continuous square tone for one enemy: the enemy you are holding with the combat target key, or with nothing held whichever enemy is nearest, whether or not you could hit it yet. Loudness alone carries distance, rising evenly from silence at the edge of its range to full at your position, while horizontal direction sets the stereo position and the far-ear delay and screen height alone sets the pitch. A held enemy sounds a perfect fifth above an unheld one, so the tone says at every moment whether what you are hearing is yours. An enemy is heard from the point of it nearest you rather than from its middle, so a worm sounds from whichever coil is closest. Distances here are fractions of your configured hostile mob tone range.

			Variants: {
				CloseRight: {
					Name: Close, on your right
					Detail: Loud and hard to your right. This is an enemy nearly on top of you.
				}

				HalfwayRight: {
					Name: Halfway to you, on your right
					Detail: The same tone at half the level. Nothing but loudness has changed, because nothing but loudness carries distance.
				}

				EdgeRight: {
					Name: At the edge of its range, on your right
					Detail: Almost silent. This is what an enemy sounds like as it first becomes audible.
				}

				CloseLeft: {
					Name: Close, on your left
					Detail: The mirror of the close right cue. Compare the two to hear how wide the stereo field is.
				}

				Above: {
					Name: Close, above you
					Detail: Height alone raises the pitch; nothing else moves it, so a high tone always means a high enemy.
				}

				Below: {
					Name: Close, below you
					Detail: The same tone dropped by the same amount it was raised above you.
				}

				Held: {
					Name: The enemy you are holding
					Detail: The close right enemy again, this time held by the combat target key: a perfect fifth above the unheld tone. It stays on that enemy however many others close in, and drops back to the lower pitch the moment the lock ends.
				}
			}
		}

		WallTones: {
			Name: Terrain wall tones
			Description: Filtered noise that draws the terrain immediately around you: one voice for each side, one for the ceiling, and one for the ground while you are falling. Each rises in pitch and in loudness as its surface gets nearer, so a corridor narrowing is heard before it is met. The sides are the neutral timbre, the ceiling is higher and narrower so it reads as thin and focused, and the floor is lower and broader so it reads as a rumble. Distances here are fractions of your configured wall tone range; because the stereo field is a fixed hundred and twenty tiles wide, a wall a few tiles away sits only slightly off centre and its timbre and pitch carry more than its pan does.

			Variants: {
				LeftClose: {
					Name: Wall close on your left
					Detail: High and loud. A surface has to answer four probes down the height of your body before it counts, so a one-tile step up stays silent.
				}

				LeftFar: {
					Name: Wall far on your left
					Detail: The same voice near the end of its range: low and quiet. Compare it with the close cue to hear the whole sweep.
				}

				RightClose: {
					Name: Wall close on your right
					Detail: The mirror of the close left cue.
				}

				Corridor: {
					Name: A narrow corridor
					Detail: Both sides close at once. Each voice sits a few decibels below the shared reference so that the pair of them together still arrives on it.
				}

				Ceiling: {
					Name: Ceiling above you
					Detail: Thinner and higher than a side. A single block overhead already blocks upward movement, so the nearest hit wins outright with no alignment required.
				}

				Floor: {
					Name: Ground below you while falling
					Detail: Broader and lower than a side. The ground is at zero distance whenever you are standing on it, so this voice only sounds mid-descent, and only if falling tones are enabled.
				}
			}
		}

		BodyBeacon: {
			Name: Freecam body beacon
			Description: While freecam has taken the camera away from your body, a short pulse every three quarters of a second marks where the body is. Once the body has left the field the pulse is projected onto the field's edge, so its direction stays readable however far the camera has travelled.

			Variants: {
				Left: {
					Name: Body to your left
					Detail: Hard left, which is where the beacon sits once the camera has carried the body past the edge of the field.
				}

				Right: {
					Name: Body to your right
					Detail: Hard right.
				}

				Above: {
					Name: Body above you
					Detail: Centred and pitched up by a tritone, which is the full vertical range of the field.
				}

				Below: {
					Name: Body below you
					Detail: Centred and pitched down by the same amount.
				}

				AtBody: {
					Name: Body at the camera
					Detail: Centred and unpitched. This is what the beacon sounds like the moment before freecam returns you.
				}
			}
		}

		BreathCues: {
			Name: Breath and submersion cues
			Description: Terraria's own splash, played alongside the spoken announcement whenever you go under, come back up, or cross another ten percent of remaining breath. It is the game's sound rather than one of Ariadne's, so it does not pass through the mod's mixer and none of Ariadne's volumes apply to it.

			Variants: {
				Submerged: {
					Name: Going under
					Detail: The full splash, played once as you submerge and again if you go under honey.
				}

				Surfaced: {
					Name: Surfacing, and each tenth of breath
					Detail: The weaker splash, played on surfacing and at every ten percent of breath lost.
				}
			}
		}
	}
}

Configs: {
	AriadneClientConfig: {
		DisplayName: Ariadne Accessibility Settings

		BiomeAnnouncementsEnabled: {
			Label: Biome announcements
			Tooltip: Announce a biome after the player has remained in it long enough to avoid rapid announcements at biome boundaries.
		}

		CursorEarconsEnabled: {
			Label: Cursor earcons
			Tooltip: Play the targeted tile or liquid's native Terraria sound for each nonempty unlocked-cursor step and each changed nonempty Smart Cursor target.
		}

		CursorEarconVolumePercent: {
			Label: Cursor earcon volume
			Tooltip: Set cursor-earcon loudness as a percentage of the game's Sound volume.
		}

		CursorCoordinateAnnouncementsEnabled: {
			Label: Cursor coordinate announcements
			Tooltip: Include tile coordinates in unlocked-cursor movement announcements. Smart Cursor never announces coordinates.
		}

		RelativeCoordinateReadoutEnabled: {
			Label: Player-relative coordinates
			Tooltip: Report positions as an offset from the player, who counts as zero, instead of as raw world tile numbers. Waypoint names always keep raw coordinates so they stay meaningful after you move.
		}

		LowHealthHeartbeatEnabled: {
			Label: Low health heartbeat
			Tooltip: Pulse a synthesized heartbeat once health falls to half, quickening it at each lower threshold down to five percent.
		}

		LowHealthHeartbeatVolumePercent: {
			Label: Low health heartbeat volume
			Tooltip: Set heartbeat loudness as a percentage of the game's Sound volume. The beat still grows louder as health falls.
		}

		LowHealthAnnouncementsEnabled: {
			Label: Low health announcements
			Tooltip: Speak the remaining health percentage each time health crosses into a lower threshold, and confirm once health has recovered.
		}

		BreathAnnouncementsEnabled: {
			Label: Breath announcements
			Tooltip: Announce submersion and each ten percent of remaining breath, paired with Terraria's own splash sound.
		}

		HotbarAnnouncementsEnabled: {
			Label: Hotbar selection announcements
			Tooltip: Speak the item and slot number whenever the selected hotbar slot changes, and again when the selected slot's contents change, such as a stack running out.
		}

		ItemPickupAnnouncementsEnabled: {
			Label: Item pickup announcements
			Tooltip: Speak each item that reaches the inventory, including fishing catches. Repeated gains of the same item are collected for a moment and reported as one running total, and coins are merged into a single value.
		}

		FootstepSoundsEnabled: {
			Label: Footstep sounds
			Tooltip: Sound a step each time the player crosses a tile boundary on foot, raised in pitch while standing on a platform.
		}

		FootstepVolumePercent: {
			Label: Footstep volume
			Tooltip: Set footstep loudness as a percentage of the game's Sound volume.
		}

		MovementBumpTonesEnabled: {
			Label: Blocked movement bumps
			Tooltip: Thud once when a walk request stops making progress, repeating slowly while the direction stays blocked. A closed door answers higher and more hollow than terrain so it does not read as a dead end. The same cue reports landings, weighted by how far you fell, and contact with a ceiling overhead.
		}

		MovementBumpVolumePercent: {
			Label: Blocked movement bump volume
			Tooltip: Set blocked-movement bump loudness as a percentage of the game's Sound volume.
		}

		WallToneEnabled: {
			Label: Wall tones
			Tooltip: Continuously indicate nearby left walls, right walls, and the gravity-relative ceiling with procedural audio. Each surface is placed where it sits on screen, and its own timbre tells a wall apart from the ceiling and the ground below you.
		}

		WallToneVolumePercent: {
			Label: Wall tone volume
			Tooltip: Set wall-tone loudness as a percentage of the game's Sound volume.
		}

		WallToneRangeTiles: {
			Label: Wall tone range
			Tooltip: Set the maximum sensing distance in tiles. A larger range begins indicating terrain sooner.
		}

		FallTonesEnabled: {
			Label: Falling tones
			Tooltip: Sound the ground below while falling, rising in pitch and loudness as it approaches. Ordinary jumps and short step-downs stay silent.
		}

		HostileMobTonesEnabled: {
			Label: Hostile and target cues
			Tooltip: Play one continuous square tone for the enemy you are holding, or for the nearest enemy on screen when nothing is held, plus a separate cue when a target is taken or lost. The tone becomes louder as that enemy approaches, screen height alone controls its pitch, and a held enemy sounds a perfect fifth higher than an unheld one.
		}

		HostileMobToneVolumePercent: {
			Label: Hostile and target cue volume
			Tooltip: Set hostile-mob tone and combat-target lock cue loudness as a percentage of the game's Sound volume.
		}

		HostileMobToneRangeTiles: {
			Label: Hostile mob tone range
			Tooltip: Set the distance in tiles at which a hostile mob tone reaches silence. Loudness rises evenly from there to full at the player, so a shorter range makes each tile of approach a larger change.
		}

		FreecamBeaconVolumePercent: {
			Label: Freecam body beacon volume
			Tooltip: Set the loudness of the periodic spatial pulse that points back to the live player while freecam is active.
		}

		RadarEnabled: {
			Label: Radar
			Tooltip: Sound a short bell from anything the scanner would list when it first comes into range, so you know when opening the scanner is worth it. It stays silent while nothing new arrives. The number of strikes says what was found, and level, stereo position and pitch place it as they do for every other spatial cue.
		}

		RadarVolumePercent: {
			Label: Radar volume
			Tooltip: Set radar ping loudness as a percentage of the game's Sound volume.
		}

		RadarRangeTiles: {
			Label: Radar range
			Tooltip: Set how far in tiles the radar reaches. A ping is quietest at the edge of that range and loudest underfoot, so a shorter range makes each tile of approach a larger change.
		}

		RadarSweepSpeechEnabled: {
			Label: Name radar contacts on a sweep
			Tooltip: Speak the nearest contacts by name when the radar sweep key is pressed. Passive discovery stays tones only either way, and an empty sweep is always reported.
		}

		RadarDetectsOresAndValuables: {
			Label: Radar finds ores and valuables
			Tooltip: Detect the ground a Spelunker Potion would highlight: ore, gems, pots and life crystals. Sounds one strike.
		}

		RadarDetectsContainers: {
			Label: Radar finds containers
			Tooltip: Detect chests, barrels and dressers. Sounds two strikes.
		}

		RadarDetectsCreatures: {
			Label: Radar finds creatures
			Tooltip: Detect the town, rescuable and passive creatures a Hunter Potion would highlight. Sounds three strikes.
		}

		RadarDetectsEnemies: {
			Label: Radar finds enemies
			Tooltip: Detect hostile creatures as well. Off by default because the hostile mob tone already follows the nearest enemy continuously. Sounds four strikes.
		}

		RadarDetectsDroppedItems: {
			Label: Radar finds dropped items
			Tooltip: Detect items lying in the world. Sounds four strikes.
		}

		RadarDetectsLiquids: {
			Label: Radar finds liquids
			Tooltip: Detect pools of water, lava, honey and shimmer. Sounds four strikes.
		}

		RadarDetectsTreesAndPlants: {
			Label: Radar finds trees and large plants
			Tooltip: Detect trees, cacti and other large plants. Sounds four strikes.
		}

		RadarDetectsPlacedObjects: {
			Label: Radar finds placed objects
			Tooltip: Detect furniture and other placed multi-tile objects. This is the busiest category indoors. Sounds four strikes.
		}

		SpatialAudioItdEnabled: {
			Label: Interaural time difference
			Tooltip: Add a small far-ear delay to wall tones, hostile and combat-target cues, the freecam body beacon, and cached native cursor earcons. Turning this off retains stereo level differences and vertical pitch.
		}

		SpatialAudioItdStrengthMilliseconds: {
			Label: Interaural time difference strength
			Tooltip: Set the global maximum far-ear delay in milliseconds. The full amount occurs at either horizontal viewport edge.
		}

		SpatialAudioHeadShadowEnabled: {
			Label: Head shadow
			Tooltip: Roll the treble off whichever ear the head is between, on top of the level difference the pan already applies. A real head takes the high frequencies and leaves the low ones. This changes the character of the stereo field, so compare it against the setting off before keeping it.
		}
	}
}
